n  open world game that they just kind of  scale down into that game just just the  thought it's interesting that's all i'm  saying another thing that's kind of  interesting to think about is at the  time of which allen week came out  survival horror was well rather kind of  on the back end of existence like the  genre as a whole had taken a churn in  for a more action-oriented focus  due in part to games like resident evil  4 and resume 5. not that i'm implying  that's why alan wake became the way it  did instead of becoming  an open world survival horror game but  considering the trend and what was  mainstream at the time  i'm not surprised it took more  action-oriented route especially  considering that  remedy entertainment was coming off the  tail end of max payne  success so that's that's it but aside  from me being a survival horror nerd as  well as a big old remedy entertainment  nerd hearing sam lake say that they're  gonna go full fledged survival horror  with this upcoming sequel just  means that it feels like it's going to  be embodying what they had intended it  to be in the first place which by the  way  again is not discounting what the game  is what the first one is in its current  state it's just there's something um  magical that's the only word that's  coming ahead to my mind  magical  um  does that even make sense  am i making sense my rambling excitement  aside i do want to talk about why i  think it makes sense for alan wake to  return  now if you finish the first game you  would know that ends with a big old  cliffhanger that kind of in my opinion  ends at a satisfying conclusion but  satisfying in the horrific way like  twilight zone or the second season of  twin peaks does now going back to that  cliffhanger the game was always  developed with a sequel in mind which  sam lake just wrote in a blog post for  playstation europe announcing alan wake  too and while following it up with a  sequel immediately didn't quite come to  fruition the way 